WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.420 --> 00:00:08.670
CHRISTINA TOSI: And I was raised by this
funny mom that loved to make box cake,

00:00:08.670 --> 00:00:10.230
and I was raised by grandmothers.

00:00:10.230 --> 00:00:13.980
And they made frosting one
way, and honestly, that

00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:17.580
is the way that we make frosting today.

00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:22.380
We don't make boiled frosting, we don't
make anything other than this frosting.

00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:29.010
And it's really simple and it's really
easy to alter the flavors of it.

00:00:29.010 --> 00:00:32.910
It's butter, unsalted
European-style butter.

00:00:32.910 --> 00:00:36.310
It's confectioners sugar.

00:00:36.310 --> 00:00:41.760
We paddle those two suckers together
until the butter has broken down.

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:48.030
We're going to make this
frosting into coffee frosting.

00:00:48.030 --> 00:00:54.480
When we are creating recipes and we're
trying to figure out what we want,

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:57.710
what flavor we want and what
vehicle-- so in this case coffee

00:00:57.710 --> 00:01:01.080
into a frosting-- we have so many
decisions we have to think about.

00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:04.680
One, what kind of what type of
frosting do we want to make?

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:10.350
Inevitably, it comes back to this
very simple style of frosting.

00:01:10.350 --> 00:01:12.840
And how are we going
to get the flavor in?

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:15.480
So when you think about coffee,
it's like, OK, we can do this.

00:01:15.480 --> 00:01:17.730
I could skin this cat
so many different ways.

00:01:17.730 --> 00:01:22.470
I can just add coffee, but
coffee is largely water-based,

00:01:22.470 --> 00:01:26.950
so it's going to all alter my volume.

00:01:26.950 --> 00:01:28.889
It's going to plummet.

00:01:28.889 --> 00:01:30.930
JENNA: It's going to
plummet the volume fraction.

00:01:30.930 --> 00:01:32.820
CHRISTINA TOSI: It's not
good, it's going to separate,

00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:34.250
it's not going to emulsify.

00:01:34.250 --> 00:01:37.650
It's not good, no one wants
an unemulsified frosting.

00:01:37.650 --> 00:01:39.149
Would you hand me one more spatula?

00:01:39.149 --> 00:01:40.872
JENNA: Yeah, of course.

00:01:40.872 --> 00:01:42.330
CHRISTINA TOSI: I can add espresso.

00:01:42.330 --> 00:01:43.140
Espresso works.

00:01:43.140 --> 00:01:45.240
Espresso is a little bit
bitter, and I don't know,

00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:47.220
I don't want it to taste like espresso.

00:01:47.220 --> 00:01:50.910
I want it to taste like coffee.

00:01:50.910 --> 00:01:52.530
What else could I do?

00:01:52.530 --> 00:01:56.490
I could melt the butter and
try and brew coffee through it,

00:01:56.490 --> 00:01:58.410
or try to do a pour over with coffee.

00:01:58.410 --> 00:02:02.160
I start going in all these places,
of like OK, how can I do it?

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:06.090
We tested so many different
recipes for coffee frosting,

00:02:06.090 --> 00:02:16.910
and we ended up taking whole milk,
instant coffee, and some kosher salt.

00:02:16.910 --> 00:02:22.440
The instant coffee really allows you
to get a depth of flavor in there

00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:27.120
without having to add too much milk.

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:31.980
So you can really, really
control the depth of flavor

00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:37.020
against the amount of milk
that you're adding in.

00:02:37.020 --> 00:02:38.760
The hardest part about
creating something

00:02:38.760 --> 00:02:41.340
is it's really easy to dream up
an idea, but it's really hard

00:02:41.340 --> 00:02:43.560
to make it into a reality.

00:02:51.240 --> 00:02:55.500
And the hardest part about
something like this coffee frosting

00:02:55.500 --> 00:02:59.430
is I know I need to add
coffee in some in some degree.

00:02:59.430 --> 00:03:04.570
The instant coffee really needs
the milk to dissolve itself.

00:03:04.570 --> 00:03:08.220
We don't-- I mean, maybe some of you eat
instant coffee granules-- but I don't.

00:03:08.220 --> 00:03:13.500
I want it to be smooth, and I want
it to have the flavor of coffee.

00:03:13.500 --> 00:03:16.080
I can't just paddle instant
coffee into my butter.

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:21.240
I need enough liquid to hydrate that
instant coffee into a coffee flavor.

00:03:21.240 --> 00:03:24.930
But I also know that I only
have a certain allowance

00:03:24.930 --> 00:03:28.320
for the amount of liquid that I can
add into this frosting before it breaks

00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:30.180
and it separates.

00:03:30.180 --> 00:03:33.270
And this coffee frosting
literally comes right up

00:03:33.270 --> 00:03:38.670
to the line in terms of keeping
that emulsification intact

00:03:38.670 --> 00:03:42.630
and separating it out.

00:03:42.630 --> 00:03:45.780
And that is the hardest
part of the job, where

00:03:45.780 --> 00:03:47.910
it's like knowing when to say when.

00:03:47.910 --> 00:03:51.990
I wish that more of
us took these classes

00:03:51.990 --> 00:03:55.524
to be able to really calculate
the math and understand.

00:03:55.524 --> 00:03:58.440
We understand why it's happening and
we understand how it's happening,

00:03:58.440 --> 00:04:04.920
but sometimes we don't understand what
the very, very, very, very, very--

00:04:04.920 --> 00:04:07.080
what the tipping point
is, and what amount

00:04:07.080 --> 00:04:10.030
we can add before it
tips over and so on.

00:04:10.030 --> 00:04:12.240
And that's the coolest
part of the job, too.

00:04:12.240 --> 00:04:15.884
It's sort of getting to learn about the
scientific process through something

00:04:15.884 --> 00:04:18.300
as simple as, all right, I
want to make a coffee frosting,

00:04:18.300 --> 00:04:21.650
but it needs to be able to
be smooth enough to spread.

